{"motion":"This House would introduce a universal basic income funded by wealth taxes.","debate_id":"mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max","setting":"multiturn","nodes":["multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::0::1360","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::0::1361","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::1::1362","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::1::1363","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::2::1364","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::2::1365","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::3::1366","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::3::1367","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::4::1368","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::4::1369","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::5::1370","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::5::1371","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::1372","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::1373","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::1374","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::1375","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::1376","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::1377","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::1378","multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::1379"],"args":[{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::0::1360","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"A universal basic income funded by wealth taxes is legally feasible under existing constitutional frameworks that permit progressive taxation and redistribution for public welfare. Wealth taxes have been upheld in various jurisdictions as consistent with property rights, provided they are not arbitrary or confiscatory. Implementing such a policy would require clear legislative drafting to ensure compliance with due process and equal protection principles, but no fundamental legal barrier exists."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::0::1361","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"While the Pro side asserts legal feasibility, wealth taxes face significant constitutional challenges in many jurisdictions, particularly where property rights are strongly protected against perceived excessive state interference. Even if structured to avoid being arbitrary, wealth taxes may still be deemed confiscatory if they significantly impair the value or use of assets, triggering legal challenges under due process or takings clauses. Furthermore, defining and valuing wealth consistently across diverse asset types raises administrative and legal complexities that could undermine the policy's enforceability and fairness."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::1::1362","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"A universal basic income funded by wealth taxes would significantly reduce poverty and economic insecurity, strengthening social cohesion and reducing inequality. It provides a stable foundation for individuals to meet basic needs, participate in society, and pursue education or caregiving without the stigma or bureaucracy of means-tested programs. This policy fosters dignity and social inclusion by ensuring everyone has a minimum level of economic security."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::1::1363","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"While the Pro side claims UBI would reduce stigma and enhance social inclusion, a universal payment funded by wealth taxes may actually erode social solidarity by decoupling benefits from contributions, weakening the perceived link between work and social support. This could foster resentment among middle- and working-class taxpayers who do not qualify for wealth taxes but still bear indirect costs through economic distortions. Additionally, universal programs may dilute support for targeted assistance that addresses the specific needs of the most vulnerable, potentially worsening social outcomes for those who need help most."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::2::1364","side":"Pro","angle":"Political","text":"From a political perspective, a universal basic income funded by wealth taxes realigns policy priorities toward greater equity and democratic accountability. It responds to rising public demand for fairer economic systems and reduces the influence of extreme wealth on political processes by redistributing resources. Such a policy strengthens the social contract by ensuring that economic gains are broadly shared, enhancing political legitimacy and stability."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::2::1365","side":"Con","angle":"Political","text":"The Pro side claims UBI would enhance political legitimacy, but in practice, funding it through wealth taxes risks deepening political polarization by framing economic policy as a zero-sum conflict between classes. Such a policy could alienate centrist and conservative voters, undermining the broad coalition-building necessary for stable governance. Moreover, concentrating redistributive power in the state may incentivize populist bidding wars, weakening institutional checks and long-term fiscal discipline."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::3::1366","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"A universal basic income funded by wealth taxes would stimulate aggregate demand by increasing the purchasing power of lower- and middle-income households, who are more likely to spend additional income. This can lead to higher economic activity and job creation, particularly in local economies. Wealth taxes also reduce the concentration of capital that can distort investment patterns and suppress competition, promoting a more efficient allocation of resources. Over time, these effects can enhance productivity and support sustainable economic growth."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::3::1367","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that UBI would stimulate demand overlooks the disincentive effects of wealth taxes on investment and capital formation. High marginal tax rates on wealth can reduce the funds available for productive investment, lowering long-term economic growth. Moreover, the administrative costs and valuation challenges of wealth taxes may lead to capital flight or asset shifting, further diminishing the tax base and offsetting any short-term demand gains."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::4::1368","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"From a moral standpoint, a universal basic income funded by wealth taxes upholds the principle that extreme wealth concentration in the presence of widespread poverty is unjust. It affirms the inherent dignity of all individuals by guaranteeing the means to meet basic needs, regardless of labor market participation. This approach corrects systemic inequities by ensuring that the benefits of societal progress are shared, not hoarded, aligning economic policy with fundamental ethical obligations to human welfare."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::4::1369","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side argues that wealth concentration amid poverty is morally unjust, but this overlooks the moral value of earned success and personal responsibility. Imposing heavy wealth taxes to fund universal payments treats individuals merely as sources of revenue rather than as autonomous agents deserving of the fruits of their labor. Such a policy undermines the moral foundation of reciprocity, where societal support is tied to mutual contribution, and risks fostering dependency rather than dignity."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::5::1370","side":"Pro","angle":"Technological","text":"Automation and artificial intelligence are rapidly displacing workers across sectors, rendering traditional employment-based safety nets increasingly inadequate. A universal basic income funded by wealth taxes directly addresses this technological disruption by providing a stable income floor as labor markets transform. It ensures that the gains from productivity-enhancing technologies, which often accrue to asset owners, are shared more broadly across society. This approach not only mitigates the economic insecurity caused by technological change but also supports a smoother transition to a more automated economy."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::5::1371","side":"Con","angle":"Technological","text":"The Pro side assumes that technological displacement justifies a universal basic income, but this overlooks how wealth taxes could stifle the very innovation driving automation. Heavy taxation on capital reduces the resources available for research, development, and deployment of new technologies, potentially slowing productivity gains and job creation in emerging sectors. Moreover, a universal payment does not address the need for reskilling or targeted support that would better prepare workers for a changing technological landscape."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::1372","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"The legal viability of a wealth tax to fund universal basic income is supported by established precedents allowing progressive taxation for public welfare, provided the tax is structured to avoid arbitrariness and respect due process. Constitutional challenges based on property rights can be mitigated through careful design, such as setting non-confiscatory rates and using transparent valuation methods. Many jurisdictions already administer complex asset-based taxes, demonstrating that legal and administrative hurdles are manageable with competent implementation."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::1373","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Con side overstates the legal intractability of wealth taxes, ignoring that constitutional systems routinely uphold progressive taxation when it serves a legitimate public purpose and adheres to procedural fairness. Challenges based on takings or due process can be addressed through clear legislative standards for asset valuation and reasonable tax rates that avoid confiscation. Existing legal frameworks already accommodate complex tax regimes, demonstrating that enforceability concerns are operational rather than fundamental."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::1374","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Wealth taxes can be designed to minimize distortions to investment by focusing on unproductive or highly concentrated assets, thereby preserving incentives for innovation while recapturing economic rents. Empirical evidence from countries with moderate wealth taxes shows limited capital flight when implementation includes robust anti-avoidance measures and international coordination. The resulting revenue can stabilize household consumption during economic downturns, reducing the need for more costly crisis interventions and supporting long-term macroeconomic resilience."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::1375","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that wealth taxes can be narrowly targeted to avoid investment distortions ignores the difficulty of distinguishing between productive and unproductive capital in practice. Even with anti-avoidance measures, wealth taxes reduce the overall capital available for investment, which can dampen long-term growth and productivity. Moreover, the assertion that such taxes stabilize consumption overlooks the potential for reduced business formation and hiring due to diminished capital accumulation, which may exacerbate economic instability rather than mitigate it."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::1376","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"The moral imperative for a universal basic income funded by wealth taxes lies in rectifying the injustice of extreme inequality, where vast fortunes coexist with preventable deprivation. It recognizes that access to basic material security is a fundamental human right, not a reward contingent on labor or contribution. By ensuring all individuals can meet their essential needs, society affirms the equal moral worth of every person, regardless of their economic status or productivity."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::1377","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Con side\u2019s emphasis on earned success and reciprocity misunderstands the moral foundation of basic income, which is not about rewarding or punishing behavior but about guaranteeing the conditions for human dignity. In a society where wealth is increasingly derived from inheritance, market luck, or structural advantages rather than individual effort, it is morally indefensible to deny basic security to those excluded from such gains. Universal basic income funded by wealth taxes corrects this imbalance by affirming that everyone, simply by virtue of being human, deserves a minimum standard of material well-being."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::1378","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"A universal basic income funded by wealth taxes promotes social stability by reducing the desperation and marginalization that fuel crime, social unrest, and political extremism. It ensures that all individuals, regardless of employment status, can participate meaningfully in community life, strengthening social trust and collective well-being. Unlike targeted welfare programs, its universality avoids the social divisions that arise from distinguishing between 'deserving' and 'undeserving' recipients."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_013_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::1379","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side claims UBI fosters social stability and trust, but universal payments funded by wealth taxes may weaken the social norm of reciprocity by severing benefits from contribution, potentially eroding public support for the welfare system. This could deepen divisions between those who pay taxes and those who receive benefits, especially if middle-income earners feel excluded from both the tax burden and the perceived gains. Moreover, by distributing resources universally rather than targeting the most marginalized, the policy may fail to address the root causes of social exclusion and inequality."}],"judges":{"GPT-5.2":[[0.0,0.78,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.03,0.0,0.12],[0.88,0.0,0.0,0.06,0.08,0.05,0.05,0.05,0.04,0.04,0.0,0.0,0.75,0.85,0.2,0.05,0.0,0.08,0.02,0.02],[0.0,0.55,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.75,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.78,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.85],[0.05,0.07,0.85,0.0,0.62,0.06,0.22,0.0,0.27,0.05,0.23,0.06,0.0,0.03,0.3,0.05,0.4,0.66,0.88,0.02],[0.0,0.47,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.58,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.7,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.8],[0.1,0.1,0.3,0.05,0.86,0.0,0.15,0.08,0.18,0.08,0.26,0.0,0.08,0.12,0.27,0.0,0.2,0.32,0.8,0.15],[0.0,0.3,0.0,0.33,0.0,0.26,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.38,0.0,0.78,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.4],[0.05,0.05,0.25,0.05,0.1,0.05,0.88,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.25,0.05,0.25,0.2,0.85,0.05,0.15,0.18,0.2,0.05],[0.0,0.3,0.0,0.75,0.0,0.5,0.0,0.55,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.55,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.75],[0.08,0.1,0.45,0.05,0.58,0.04,0.32,0.18,0.88,0.0,0.42,0.08,0.08,0.07,0.42,0.0,0.87,0.9,0.62,0.0],[0.0,0.76,0.0,0.66,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.72,0.0,0.78,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.78,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.72],[0.14,0.08,0.3,0.05,0.28,0.05,0.75,0.0,0.32,0.05,0.6,0.0,0.12,0.1,0.65,0.05,0.3,0.35,0.3,0.05],[0.0,0.85,0.0,0.08,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.35,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.15],[0.0,0.85,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.06,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.04,0.03,0.05,0.0,0.0,0.03,0.0,0.0,0.06,0.0,0.0],[0.0,0.58,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.52,0.03,0.6,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.9,0.05,0.05,0.03,0.22],[0.0,0.06,0.08,0.05,0.08,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.1,0.0,0.15,0.05,0.05,0.05,0.9,0.0,0.15,0.1,0.2,0.05],[0.0,0.33,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.4,0.0,0.42,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.02,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.65],[0.0,0.45,0.0,0.65,0.0,0.